1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
From time immemorial, humans have been developing weapons

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:09,240
that can blast through, break down,

3
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:12,240
and breach the world's strongest fortifications.

4
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:19,160
From the ancient battering ramp to medieval catapults

5
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:25,260
and crossbows, from cannons and tanks

6
00:00:25,261 --> 00:00:30,260
to computer-guided cruise missiles.

7
00:00:30,261 --> 00:00:35,260
Now, Siege Machines on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:58,500
King Edward had one.

9
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:01,200
Napoleon had one.

10
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,740
And so does Hugh Kennedy.

11
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:08,740
Early on in my youth, I read about these large siege

12
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:12,920
machines, and I was rather more than fascinated by the idea

13
00:01:13,020 --> 00:01:15,820
of building one sometime, particularly when I heard

14
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:19,020
that the emperor Napoleon III had tried to build one in the

15
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:23,180
1850s and had not really succeeded very well.

16
00:01:23,181 --> 00:01:27,620
Hugh Kennedy, a wealthy English landowner and military historian,

17
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:30,780
owns the only full-size trebuchet,

18
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:34,820
a giant seesaw-like device in existence today.

19
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:38,820
A trebuchet is an engine of war.

20
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:43,540
It's used for throwing large weights.

21
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:46,840
In the late 12th and early 13th centuries,

22
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:51,480
siege machines similar to this four-story, 30-ton instrument

23
00:01:51,481 --> 00:01:56,480
instrument of destruction, were used to demolish fortress walls at will.

24
00:01:56,481 --> 00:02:02,480
The biggest ones were capable of throwing a rock maybe weighing 600 pounds.

25
00:02:02,481 --> 00:02:05,480
And with a rock of that size, you can do quite a bit of damage.

26
00:02:05,481 --> 00:02:09,480
The trebuchet was fearsome because it was repeatable and it had awesome power.

27
00:02:09,481 --> 00:02:13,480
The most powerful thing, the most powerful machine on the planet at the time.

28
00:02:13,481 --> 00:02:18,480
And we do have instructions drafted in the late 13th century

29
00:02:18,481 --> 00:02:21,020
for the construction of a trebuchet.

30
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:24,620
And they begin with the words, he who would build a trebuchet

31
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:27,820
must take care.

32
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:30,720
Guided by rare 19th century drawings,

33
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:35,540
Kennedy designed and built his own trebuchet.

34
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:38,840
The drawings will tell you the principle on which they worked,

35
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:40,540
but that's all.

36
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:44,580
We do know that they would heave boulders a certain distance.

37
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:47,180
And these boulders had certain impact characteristics

38
00:02:47,181 --> 00:02:49,820
characteristics when they hit.

39
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:52,680
Working backwards from that, and also knowing

40
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:54,920
what kind of woodworking skills they had,

41
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:57,060
what kind of materials they had at hand,

42
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:59,500
and applying our understanding of engineering today,

43
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:02,260
we're able to reconstruct what we think those machines were

44
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:04,600
like.

45
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:06,940
Unlike his medieval mentors who employed

46
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:09,900
the massive machine to knock down castle walls

47
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,640
during a siege, Kennedy must settle for the simple pleasure

48
00:03:13,641 --> 00:03:17,480
things, big things through the air, in this case,

49
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:19,380
a 450-pound piano.

50
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:22,040
The excitement of this type of technology,

51
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:23,980
as with many types of military technologies,

52
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:25,880
as long as you're not being hit, it's

53
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:27,580
pretty doggone exciting to watch.

54
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,300
It's so graceful.

55
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:35,400
It starts almost imperceptibly.

56
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:38,100
It's very slow at first, and then really gracefully

57
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:39,840
it moves around in this huge arc.

58
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:42,040
And nothing's put it there but human effort.

59
00:03:42,041 --> 00:03:45,340
but human effort, and it's just quite an amazing thing to see.

60
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:50,960
People, even now, they just burst into spontaneous applause

61
00:03:50,961 --> 00:03:52,140
when the trebuchet goes.

62
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:01,720
The trebuchet is one of the most formidable

63
00:04:01,721 --> 00:04:03,380
and impressive siege machines.

64
00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:06,260
But what exactly are siege machines?

65
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:09,520
A siege machine is anything, actually,

66
00:04:09,521 --> 00:04:14,560
that helps an army sitting around a castle or a city to take it.

67
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:16,760
It's almost always made of wood

68
00:04:16,761 --> 00:04:20,120
because in the Middle Ages, wood was the chief material

69
00:04:20,121 --> 00:04:21,780
used for almost any structure.

70
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:25,380
But siege machines come in all set shapes and sizes.

71
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:28,000
A generic term for a siege machine

72
00:04:28,001 --> 00:04:31,960
is something that will destroy that fortification,

73
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:34,120
that will cause a breach in that fortification

74
00:04:34,121 --> 00:04:37,720
so that armies may intrude on the defenders

75
00:04:37,721 --> 00:04:40,720
and defeat it and conquer it.

76
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:43,820
Siege machines have evolved from man's first long-range

77
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:47,260
projectile-throwing device, the slingshot,

78
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:50,660
to the computer-driven cruise missiles of today.

79
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:53,160
Oh, yeah.

80
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:55,480
It is the primitive mechanics of the machines

81
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:57,880
used in the Middle Ages, however,

82
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:00,080
that provide us with the most surprising look

83
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:02,080
at their development.

84
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:03,880
A lot of people have a vision of the past

85
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:06,580
that people did not have very high levels of skill

86
00:05:06,581 --> 00:05:08,533
skill in engineering and technology.

87
00:05:08,534 --> 00:05:11,300
And what machines like these prove is that they did

88
00:05:11,301 --> 00:05:14,265
have very great engineering skills.

89
00:05:14,266 --> 00:05:18,500
People who do not understand the laws of nature in

90
00:05:18,501 --> 00:05:24,500
the way that we understand them, nonetheless by trial and error are working out the principle

91
00:05:24,501 --> 00:05:26,743
of the lever. That's the central principle.

92
00:05:26,744 --> 00:05:29,300
The engineers of old would, they would apprentice

93
00:05:29,301 --> 00:05:31,375
with other engineers and learn their skills.

94
00:05:31,376 --> 00:05:33,780
They would spend a lifetime developing an intuitive

95
00:05:33,781 --> 00:05:40,280
of materials and of strength and of force and leverage.

96
00:05:40,380 --> 00:05:43,420
Ron Toms knows all about force and leverage

97
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:47,120
because he too spends much of his life building siege machines.

98
00:05:47,220 --> 00:05:49,820
I fell in love with the engineering behind siege

99
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:51,740
engines and trebuchets.

100
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:53,940
In medieval times, these were military weapons.

101
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:56,240
These were secret.

102
00:05:56,340 --> 00:05:59,540
These were the state of the art for military weaponry.

103
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:01,940
And as such, the plans for these things,

104
00:06:01,941 --> 00:06:04,580
for these things, the detailed descriptions of how they work

105
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:07,780
are not generally available.

106
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:10,180
Tom's has developed an intuitive understanding

107
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:13,120
of medieval machines and the power they wield

108
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:16,760
by building trebuchets and catapults the old fashioned way,

109
00:06:16,860 --> 00:06:17,760
trial and error.

110
00:06:17,860 --> 00:06:19,860
3, 2, 1.

111
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:28,160
Even these scaled down machines have a force

112
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:29,900
to be reckoned with.

113
00:06:29,901 --> 00:06:36,560
Now to get a 10 or 12 pound object going 70 or 80 miles an hour in about a third of a second,

114
00:06:36,820 --> 00:06:40,880
which is how long it takes for the trebuchet to actually fire, that's a lot of acceleration,

115
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:41,780
that's a lot of power.

116
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:45,660
And that's one of the things that made the trebuchet so successful and so fascinating

117
00:06:45,661 --> 00:06:47,740
is just how much power is capable.

118
00:06:47,741 --> 00:06:49,240
It's not cool.

119
00:06:59,180 --> 00:07:01,980
Much less sophisticated than the medieval trebuchet,

120
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:06,760
but successful in its own way, was the ancient battering ram.

121
00:07:06,860 --> 00:07:09,560
The battering ram consisted of a whole tree trunk,

122
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:11,840
reinforced with a metal head, which

123
00:07:11,940 --> 00:07:16,240
was thrust repeatedly against a wall or gate.

124
00:07:16,241 --> 00:07:18,880
individuals with a big, huge tree trunk between them,

125
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:22,080
they may be very effective as long as there are no defenders

126
00:07:22,180 --> 00:07:24,180
who are firing down upon them.

127
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:25,680
So again, you have to protect them.

128
00:07:25,780 --> 00:07:27,780
And pretty soon, what you begin to see

129
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:33,000
are wheeled battering rams with covers over the top of them.

130
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:35,400
Suspended from an overhead beam,

131
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:37,760
the battering ram was rolled up to its target

132
00:07:37,860 --> 00:07:40,800
for prolonged attack.

133
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:43,240
Rams on rollers were an essential component

134
00:07:43,340 --> 00:07:46,240
of Roman siege arsenals as early as the 4th century

135
00:07:46,241 --> 00:07:50,980
century BC. While all siege machines rely

136
00:07:50,981 --> 00:07:54,680
on the use of force, not all of them are quite so simple.

137
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:04,200
While Hollywood has a way of marketing ancient and medieval

138
00:08:04,201 --> 00:08:09,600
mayhem, the images belie the deadly nature of siege machines.

139
00:08:09,601 --> 00:08:12,040
Attack and defensive fortifications are two of the

140
00:08:12,041 --> 00:08:14,840
most essential components of warfare.

141
00:08:14,841 --> 00:08:18,940
Each side employs machines capable of breaching barriers,

142
00:08:18,941 --> 00:08:24,180
ultimately forcing the other side to surrender.

143
00:08:24,181 --> 00:08:27,980
Siege warfare, the art of conquering a town or castle,

144
00:08:27,981 --> 00:08:30,520
posed special problems for military engineers

145
00:08:30,521 --> 00:08:32,460
during the Middle Ages.

146
00:08:32,461 --> 00:08:34,800
The point of a siege is that a castle controls

147
00:08:34,801 --> 00:08:36,200
the countryside around it.

148
00:08:36,201 --> 00:08:39,700
If you want to take over a country, take over a land,

149
00:08:39,701 --> 00:08:43,100
you've got to take the castle.

150
00:08:43,101 --> 00:08:46,740
But taking a castle by medieval means wasn't easy.

151
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:50,440
The problem with the castle is that you can't get into it

152
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:54,180
without either battering a hole in the wall,

153
00:08:54,280 --> 00:08:57,040
knocking the gate down, or climbing over.

154
00:08:57,140 --> 00:08:59,520
And your big problem is that the people on the walls

155
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:01,020
won't let you do that.

156
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:03,380
Well, in order then to defeat these fortifications that

157
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:05,920
have been around for 4,000 years,

158
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:12,460
man devised methods by which to bring the fortifications down.

159
00:09:12,461 --> 00:09:14,300
The ingenuity of these siege machines

160
00:09:14,301 --> 00:09:16,900
developed in direct relationship to the complexity

161
00:09:16,901 --> 00:09:19,640
of the fortifications they faced.

162
00:09:19,641 --> 00:09:23,940
Perhaps the most primitive siege machine was the ladder.

163
00:09:23,941 --> 00:09:25,500
All the way through the earliest period

164
00:09:25,501 --> 00:09:27,440
of the history of warfare, the ladder

165
00:09:27,441 --> 00:09:31,940
remains the strongest, best siege machine that is around.

166
00:09:31,941 --> 00:09:34,080
It's also the cheapest to make.

167
00:09:34,081 --> 00:09:35,960
For example, Joan of Arc testifies

168
00:09:35,961 --> 00:09:40,220
that she put her ladder onto the fortification of Orleans

169
00:09:40,221 --> 00:09:42,120
and she went up that ladder first.

170
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:43,420
But that's the problem.

171
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:46,120
Who goes up the ladder first is generally

172
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:48,920
going to be met with the strongest defense

173
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:50,760
against that ladder.

174
00:09:50,860 --> 00:09:53,560
If you can dominate the castle with firepower,

175
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:55,660
make the defenders keep their heads down,

176
00:09:55,760 --> 00:10:00,380
then a lot of ladders go up in a fairly limited stretch of wall.

177
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:01,780
Somebody is going to get over.

178
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:04,620
That's how that works.

179
00:10:04,720 --> 00:10:06,520
While bows and arrows were capable

180
00:10:06,620 --> 00:10:09,920
of delivering deadly blows, soldiers on leg

181
00:10:09,921 --> 00:10:13,220
ladders were completely vulnerable.

182
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:16,120
That's when armies began to develop specialized ladders

183
00:10:16,220 --> 00:10:18,560
with wheels on them.

184
00:10:18,660 --> 00:10:21,100
Covered in protective hides, these machines

185
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:23,340
were called siege towers.

186
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:25,560
You could have a large number of troops already

187
00:10:25,660 --> 00:10:28,240
in the siege tower as you rolled it up.

188
00:10:28,340 --> 00:10:30,940
Because it was protected all the way down to the ground,

189
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:32,380
you had a constant number of troops

190
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:35,080
then that were ready to go up the ladder.

191
00:10:35,180 --> 00:10:37,720
In a way, it's the Trojan horse on the outside

192
00:10:37,820 --> 00:10:38,620
of the fortification.

193
00:10:38,621 --> 00:10:40,660
You're delivering a large number of individuals

194
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:45,900
into the town for a heated blow until you can open it up

195
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,260
and allow more individuals in.

196
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:51,940
The largest siege tower of ancient times

197
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:57,500
is the Hellepolis, built in Athens in 304 BC.

198
00:10:57,600 --> 00:11:00,600
The tower was reinforced with iron plates on three sides

199
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:02,680
as a protection from fire.

200
00:11:02,780 --> 00:11:06,080
Almost nine stories high, weighing approximately 150

201
00:11:06,081 --> 00:11:09,320
the tower was mounted on giant casters

202
00:11:09,420 --> 00:11:14,320
and propelled, presumably in relays, by 3,400 men.

203
00:11:14,420 --> 00:11:17,800
Artillery ports on all levels contained battering rams,

204
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:20,560
archers, and a variety of missile-throwing devices

205
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:22,560
known as catapults.

206
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:24,800
By the time we moved to catapults in history,

207
00:11:24,900 --> 00:11:27,200
we've taken the siege in a different direction.

208
00:11:27,300 --> 00:11:31,280
Now no longer is it simply man against the fortification.

209
00:11:31,380 --> 00:11:34,900
Now it is man's tool against the fortification.

210
00:11:34,901 --> 00:11:36,700
of the fortification.

211
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:40,200
Catapult is a generic term for all ancient and medieval

212
00:11:40,300 --> 00:11:42,740
missile-throwing siege machines.

213
00:11:42,840 --> 00:11:45,040
The first catapult appeared in ancient Greece

214
00:11:45,140 --> 00:11:47,240
in the fourth century BC.

215
00:11:47,340 --> 00:11:49,940
All of these machines operate on the basic principle

216
00:11:50,040 --> 00:11:52,360
of using various mechanical devices

217
00:11:52,460 --> 00:11:55,560
to store and release greater amounts of energy.

218
00:11:55,660 --> 00:11:58,760
To attack a castle, you needed missiles above all.

219
00:11:58,860 --> 00:12:02,560
And the basic problem you've got is that everything that you

220
00:12:02,561 --> 00:12:05,560
they can do back at you, only better,

221
00:12:05,561 --> 00:12:08,560
because they've got height and therefore range.

222
00:12:08,561 --> 00:12:11,560
So how do you respond to that?

223
00:12:11,561 --> 00:12:13,560
You build big machines.

224
00:12:13,561 --> 00:12:18,560
Machines, as time goes by, become more and more elaborate.

225
00:12:18,561 --> 00:12:21,560
They can throw heavier and heavier stones.

226
00:12:21,561 --> 00:12:23,560
There's three types of siege engine.

227
00:12:23,561 --> 00:12:29,560
There's the tension machine, in which the bow is bent back

228
00:12:29,561 --> 00:12:33,800
to provide the force to throw a bolt.

229
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:37,000
An early tension machine introduced in Syracuse

230
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:39,800
was the gastrophetes, or belly bow.

231
00:12:39,900 --> 00:12:43,800
It added, on average, 81 yards to the flight of the projectile

232
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:47,320
over that of a traditional bow and arrow.

233
00:12:47,420 --> 00:12:48,840
Then we have the torsion machine,

234
00:12:48,940 --> 00:12:51,880
where the energy is stored in a skein of rope, which is then

235
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:54,120
twisted very, very tightly.

236
00:12:54,220 --> 00:12:59,560
And as the arm comes back, it gets tighter.

237
00:12:59,561 --> 00:13:05,160
One early type of torsion machine was the bolt shooter,

238
00:13:05,260 --> 00:13:10,700
or oxybellus, which appeared around 375 BC and fired

239
00:13:10,800 --> 00:13:15,100
bolts, including 27-inch arrows with iron tips.

240
00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:18,840
The force of the bolt could pierce a man's shield and armor

241
00:13:18,940 --> 00:13:23,020
at ranges in excess of a quarter mile.

242
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:25,880
Another torsion machine was the stone thrower,

243
00:13:25,980 --> 00:13:29,560
or lithabolos, which could fire rocks from two

244
00:13:29,561 --> 00:13:33,560
to 180 pounds. Such machines were generally used

245
00:13:33,660 --> 00:13:36,560
at point-blank range and were capable of stripping

246
00:13:36,660 --> 00:13:39,000
battlements from fortified walls.

247
00:13:39,100 --> 00:13:40,700
And then the third type of engine

248
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:44,500
is the counterpoise engine, which, if you imagine,

249
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:50,720
is a bit like a seesaw with the center set off to one side.

250
00:13:50,820 --> 00:13:53,720
These ancient siege machines were not only hard to move

251
00:13:53,820 --> 00:13:57,620
around in battle, but also wildly inaccurate.

252
00:13:57,621 --> 00:14:00,120
And this is where we get the development of the crossbow,

253
00:14:00,220 --> 00:14:04,420
the notion that you can actually use a wood or bone

254
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:09,360
or composite bow arms, and then eventually metal, steel

255
00:14:09,460 --> 00:14:14,440
primarily, and that if you can draw that string back far

256
00:14:14,540 --> 00:14:17,740
enough, the energy that can be delivered to this

257
00:14:17,840 --> 00:14:20,380
can penetrate almost anything.

258
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:23,920
Unlike the stiff wood bows used in ancient times,

259
00:14:23,921 --> 00:14:27,120
the medieval crossbow was a smaller, more flexible,

260
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:29,460
and accurate tension machine.

261
00:14:29,560 --> 00:14:33,160
So accurate that it was both loathed and popular

262
00:14:33,260 --> 00:14:34,460
as a siege engine.

263
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:36,640
There's only one weapon system that was ever banned

264
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:39,360
by the pope, and that's the crossbow.

265
00:14:39,460 --> 00:14:42,380
The crossbow was banned by the pope for one simple reason.

266
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:44,400
They figured it was unfair.

267
00:14:44,500 --> 00:14:46,380
OK, it was too strong.

268
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:48,380
It could be fired by individuals who

269
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:50,720
are less skilled than the archer.

270
00:14:50,820 --> 00:14:53,920
It could be fired by almost anybody.

271
00:14:53,921 --> 00:14:57,520
And it becomes a very interesting development

272
00:14:57,521 --> 00:15:01,320
of technology over skill.

273
00:15:01,321 --> 00:15:05,660
Introduced into England by the Norman invaders in 1066,

274
00:15:05,661 --> 00:15:08,040
the crossbow was attached to a stock

275
00:15:08,041 --> 00:15:11,340
and could be kept loaded without effort from the soldier.

276
00:15:11,341 --> 00:15:13,680
The stock allowed the bending and release of the bow

277
00:15:13,681 --> 00:15:18,540
to be mechanically assisted, increasing its ballistic force.

278
00:15:18,541 --> 00:15:20,480
With the addition of sophisticated cocking

279
00:15:20,481 --> 00:15:23,320
mechanisms in the 13th and 14th centuries,

280
00:15:23,321 --> 00:15:27,320
to draw back the bow with ease, the crossbow fired a missile

281
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:32,560
a minute with a maximum range of 380 yards, 100 yards further

282
00:15:32,660 --> 00:15:33,760
than the ancient bow.

283
00:15:39,940 --> 00:15:43,000
Built in the late 13th century, Carfilly Castle

284
00:15:43,100 --> 00:15:47,180
is one of the great strongholds of medieval Europe.

285
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:50,120
Located in Wales, Carfilly is an example

286
00:15:50,121 --> 00:15:52,460
of how medieval fortifications adapted

287
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:57,760
to the increasingly powerful siege machines that attacked them.

288
00:15:57,860 --> 00:15:59,520
WALLS BECOME BETTER.

289
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:01,460
Towers are crucial.

290
00:16:01,560 --> 00:16:04,360
Towers begin square because it's easiest

291
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:06,700
to build a big building square.

292
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:09,240
But the trouble with squares is that the corners hide

293
00:16:09,340 --> 00:16:11,280
people approaching.

294
00:16:11,380 --> 00:16:15,780
So you build round towers where you have absolutely perfect

295
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:18,940
vision all around, and the arrow slits, of course,

296
00:16:18,941 --> 00:16:23,940
of course, also have accordingly better vision.

297
00:16:24,040 --> 00:16:27,080
The most intriguing feature of Carfilly Castle, however,

298
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:31,880
is its multiple lines of defense, walls within walls.

299
00:16:31,980 --> 00:16:34,780
That's the principle of the concentric castle.

300
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:38,260
That is, one line of defense is supported

301
00:16:38,360 --> 00:16:40,860
by another line of defense.

302
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:45,440
So that if the enemy break into the first line of defense,

303
00:16:45,540 --> 00:16:48,700
if they can get in, then they face

304
00:16:48,701 --> 00:16:54,000
a second line of defense so that every obstacle, there's

305
00:16:54,100 --> 00:16:55,500
another obstacle.

306
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:59,340
You never, as it were, get to the end until you really get to

307
00:16:59,440 --> 00:17:02,940
the middle of the castle.

308
00:17:03,040 --> 00:17:05,220
For an attacking army in the Middle Ages to be

309
00:17:05,320 --> 00:17:08,580
successful, they had to build the biggest and best siege

310
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:10,960
machines money could buy.

311
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:14,760
Four scale down machines, all fully operational, are on

312
00:17:14,860 --> 00:17:16,860
display at Carr Filly.

313
00:17:16,960 --> 00:17:18,060
Please.

314
00:17:18,061 --> 00:17:23,060
MISTER, MANGONEL, TREBOUCHER,

315
00:17:26,940 --> 00:17:30,300
AND TRACTION TREBOUCHER, OR PERIER.

316
00:17:36,740 --> 00:17:40,980
These machines are babies in comparison to what could have been achieved.

317
00:17:40,981 --> 00:17:43,820
The size and the scale of the machines here at Kefili Castle

318
00:17:43,821 --> 00:17:47,260
is based on what we can use safely within the grounds of the castle.

319
00:17:48,060 --> 00:17:51,260
The ballista is, in effect, a giant crossbow.

320
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:53,260
Originally developed by the ancient Greeks

321
00:17:53,360 --> 00:17:56,700
during the siege of Syracuse around 399 BC,

322
00:17:56,800 --> 00:18:02,300
this machine was used primarily as an anti-personnel weapon.

323
00:18:02,400 --> 00:18:06,620
It uses two vertically mounted skeins of rope,

324
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:08,520
as you can see here.

325
00:18:08,620 --> 00:18:11,420
And unlike a normal crossbow, you'll

326
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:13,320
see that the arms are solid.

327
00:18:13,420 --> 00:18:15,420
There's no bend in those at all.

328
00:18:15,520 --> 00:18:17,020
As the string is pulled back, you

329
00:18:17,021 --> 00:18:20,860
you can see those skeins starting to twist.

330
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:22,760
Now, the farther you pull it back,

331
00:18:22,860 --> 00:18:26,100
the more tension you build in those skeins,

332
00:18:26,200 --> 00:18:29,100
so that eventually, when you get it all the way back

333
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:33,000
to the trigger here and hooked over,

334
00:18:33,100 --> 00:18:36,000
it's that that gives the engine its propulsive force.

335
00:18:36,100 --> 00:18:38,800
The whole thing is mounted on a kind of crude universal joint

336
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:42,140
at this end, which allows you to move it up

337
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:45,340
and from side to side.

338
00:18:45,341 --> 00:18:48,680
This half-ton ballista fires deadly darts or bolts

339
00:18:48,780 --> 00:18:51,740
made of beaten brass or bronze.

340
00:18:51,840 --> 00:18:54,620
The objective here is that this part

341
00:18:54,720 --> 00:18:57,560
punches through the armor, and it's actually wider here

342
00:18:57,660 --> 00:19:00,320
than the rest of the shaft, so that that allows,

343
00:19:00,420 --> 00:19:01,920
once the head's gone through armor,

344
00:19:02,020 --> 00:19:04,500
for the rest of the shaft to carry on through the hole

345
00:19:04,600 --> 00:19:06,020
that the head's made.

346
00:19:06,120 --> 00:19:09,000
And this is an armor-piercing anti-personnel weapon,

347
00:19:09,100 --> 00:19:10,660
very effective against an armored knight,

348
00:19:10,760 --> 00:19:11,940
especially at close range.

349
00:19:15,340 --> 00:19:18,340
Behind us, we have a mangonel, otherwise known as an oniga,

350
00:19:18,440 --> 00:19:19,740
which is the Latin for a wild ass,

351
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:22,380
because it has a kick like a mule when it goes off.

352
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:24,580
It's probably of late classical origin.

353
00:19:24,680 --> 00:19:27,080
It's a single-armed torsion engine.

354
00:19:27,180 --> 00:19:31,580
It would have been used to attack either lines of infantry

355
00:19:31,680 --> 00:19:35,200
or a defensive position.

356
00:19:35,300 --> 00:19:38,600
A mangonel weighed between 2 and 6 tons

357
00:19:38,700 --> 00:19:41,400
and was disassembled for transport and reassembled

358
00:19:41,500 --> 00:19:42,700
for a siege.

359
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:45,340
It was not a rapid-fire machine, but a mangonel

360
00:19:45,341 --> 00:19:48,380
but did have limited success in pitching incendiaries

361
00:19:48,480 --> 00:19:49,720
with a spoon-like arm.

362
00:19:49,820 --> 00:19:51,080
Better loose.

363
00:19:51,180 --> 00:19:52,280
Loose.

364
00:19:56,480 --> 00:19:57,980
People tried to solve the problem of how

365
00:19:58,080 --> 00:19:59,860
to throw bigger things.

366
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:02,360
What they were really trying to throw was flaming pots of tar

367
00:20:02,460 --> 00:20:03,960
and this thing called Greek fire,

368
00:20:04,060 --> 00:20:06,600
which is a mixture of chemicals that, when you throw water on it,

369
00:20:06,700 --> 00:20:08,560
it only makes the fire more intense.

370
00:20:08,660 --> 00:20:14,600
It was a flammable liquid that was then sent out

371
00:20:14,601 --> 00:20:17,540
But again, it was not used very often because of the danger,

372
00:20:17,640 --> 00:20:20,340
and also, I think, because of the lack of material.

373
00:20:20,440 --> 00:20:22,600
Problem is, is it seems to have been rather secret,

374
00:20:22,700 --> 00:20:24,540
and we don't have a lot of evidence in it.

375
00:20:28,140 --> 00:20:30,920
Depending on the weight and type of projectile,

376
00:20:31,020 --> 00:20:34,220
the range for the mangonel and the ballista at Carr Philly

377
00:20:34,320 --> 00:20:35,620
is about 150 yards.

378
00:20:44,600 --> 00:20:47,900
The largest and most effective siege machine ever invented

379
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:51,440
is the trebuchet, which according to medieval experts,

380
00:20:51,540 --> 00:20:54,240
holds the distinction of being the most powerful form

381
00:20:54,340 --> 00:20:56,540
of mechanical artillery ever devised.

382
00:20:59,440 --> 00:21:03,060
Just the sight of Edward I's 14th century trebuchet

383
00:21:03,160 --> 00:21:06,760
was enough to force defenders to surrender.

384
00:21:06,860 --> 00:21:09,900
It's worth remembering the fact that for 200 years in Europe,

385
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,700
this was the most powerful weapon in the world.

386
00:21:12,701 --> 00:21:16,600
There was nothing more powerful than this.

387
00:21:16,700 --> 00:21:19,040
Trebuchets like those at Carfilly Castle

388
00:21:19,140 --> 00:21:23,040
revolutionized siege warfare, not only because of the range

389
00:21:23,140 --> 00:21:26,240
of their destructive power, but also because of their accuracy.

390
00:21:34,260 --> 00:21:36,260
These machines are very accurate.

391
00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:38,500
Provided you keep the missiles perfectly round

392
00:21:38,600 --> 00:21:41,100
and exactly the same weight, and you don't touch anything,

393
00:21:41,101 --> 00:21:44,340
it will hit the same spot time and time again.

394
00:21:44,440 --> 00:21:45,840
Now, that was necessary, actually,

395
00:21:45,940 --> 00:21:48,840
because in order to even hope to penetrate one

396
00:21:48,940 --> 00:21:52,240
of these massive walls, you needed to keep hitting the spot

397
00:21:52,340 --> 00:21:53,240
time and time again.

398
00:21:56,440 --> 00:21:58,760
The trebuchet draws its remarkable power

399
00:21:58,860 --> 00:22:02,060
not from twisted ropes, but from gravity.

400
00:22:02,160 --> 00:22:04,800
At the short end of this giant seesaw-like engine

401
00:22:04,900 --> 00:22:07,300
is a counterweight. Well, this one here

402
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:10,600
has got about two tons in the counterbalance box.

403
00:22:10,601 --> 00:22:12,040
It's sand and rock.

404
00:22:12,460 --> 00:22:13,620
We know lead was used.

405
00:22:13,980 --> 00:22:16,880
Now, lead's much more dense.

406
00:22:17,340 --> 00:22:20,940
And for a cubic meter of lead, it weighs about 11 tons.

407
00:22:21,380 --> 00:22:23,600
So if we filled that with lead, it would be a hell of a lot more

408
00:22:23,601 --> 00:22:24,600
heavier than it is.

409
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:31,680
At Carfilly, a crew of seven to nine is needed to pull down

410
00:22:31,681 --> 00:22:33,920
the 20-foot arm and cock the counterweight.

411
00:22:34,560 --> 00:22:39,140
A 24-pound concrete missile is then placed in the 30-foot sling.

412
00:22:40,600 --> 00:22:46,600
Because the counterweight is closer to the fulcrum,

413
00:22:46,700 --> 00:22:50,540
as it drops, the longer end of the arm swings around,

414
00:22:50,640 --> 00:22:53,540
propelling the concrete missile at tremendous speeds.

415
00:23:00,660 --> 00:23:04,660
I have been party to an experiment where a laser gun

416
00:23:04,760 --> 00:23:10,580
was used to track a missile, and we clocked it in a

417
00:23:10,600 --> 00:23:18,600
125 miles an hour, and that was a 350-pound sandstone ball.

418
00:23:18,601 --> 00:23:21,040
A smaller, more mobile version of the trebuchet

419
00:23:21,041 --> 00:23:25,120
originated in Asia in the 10th century.

420
00:23:25,121 --> 00:23:27,880
It's a simple machine, much like our Perrier,

421
00:23:27,881 --> 00:23:32,680
where the pulling power of a team of people on one end of an arm

422
00:23:32,681 --> 00:23:36,000
pulls down a weight at the other end and shoots it off.

423
00:23:36,001 --> 00:23:38,520
That's a Perrier, or traction trebuchet.

424
00:23:38,521 --> 00:23:40,600
Go!

425
00:23:40,601 --> 00:23:43,740
They were very easy to transport.

426
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:45,340
They were quick to make.

427
00:23:45,440 --> 00:23:49,100
And you could use a large crew of men to operate them.

428
00:23:49,200 --> 00:23:51,240
So it's quite typical that you would have

429
00:23:51,340 --> 00:23:53,340
had five or six of these in a row, maybe,

430
00:23:53,440 --> 00:23:55,740
attacking any given section of wall.

431
00:23:55,840 --> 00:23:59,660
The early traction trebuchets fired projectiles weighing

432
00:23:59,760 --> 00:24:04,860
up to 130 pounds at a distance between 90 and 145 yards.

433
00:24:04,861 --> 00:24:11,200
up to 130 pounds at a distance between 90 and 145 yards.

434
00:24:11,300 --> 00:24:14,300
The real advantage, however, is that they could fire five or six

435
00:24:14,400 --> 00:24:17,200
times a minute.

436
00:24:17,300 --> 00:24:20,400
By the 13th century, the trebuchet displaced all other

437
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:24,820
siege machines and continued to hold its own for the next 200

438
00:24:24,920 --> 00:24:28,720
years, despite the growing popularity of gunpowder.

439
00:24:28,721 --> 00:24:36,720
Harnessing the explosive force necessary to propel a projectile

440
00:24:36,820 --> 00:24:40,160
would be the vital step in the escalation of siege machine

441
00:24:40,260 --> 00:24:42,260
technology.

442
00:24:42,360 --> 00:24:46,100
But the invention of the cannon was dependent on gunpowder.

443
00:24:46,200 --> 00:24:49,080
We're not exactly sure when gunpowder is invented.

444
00:24:49,180 --> 00:24:52,240
It does appear that it was invented in China somewhere

445
00:24:52,340 --> 00:24:55,520
before the 10th century AD.

446
00:24:55,521 --> 00:24:59,760
It also seems to have gone to the West.

447
00:24:59,860 --> 00:25:02,800
What's distinctive about the Western use of it

448
00:25:02,900 --> 00:25:05,500
is the almost immediate attempt at the very end

449
00:25:05,600 --> 00:25:09,240
of the 13th century to put it in the tube

450
00:25:09,340 --> 00:25:13,600
and use it to fire balls of some sort or other,

451
00:25:13,700 --> 00:25:15,740
usually at fortifications.

452
00:25:15,840 --> 00:25:18,240
You have to realize that it had been known in the Arab world

453
00:25:18,340 --> 00:25:22,020
for well over 100 years and hadn't been used like that

454
00:25:22,120 --> 00:25:23,940
and had been used for hundreds of years

455
00:25:23,941 --> 00:25:25,940
for different purposes in China,

456
00:25:25,941 --> 00:25:27,940
but they had never, as it were,

457
00:25:27,941 --> 00:25:31,940
sought the development of the cannon

458
00:25:31,941 --> 00:25:33,940
in the way that the West did.

459
00:25:33,941 --> 00:25:37,940
Roger Bacon, who was considered to be one of the great scientists

460
00:25:37,941 --> 00:25:39,940
of the Middle Ages, he says,

461
00:25:39,941 --> 00:25:43,940
if you could take this gunpowder, which is a combination of carbon,

462
00:25:43,941 --> 00:25:47,940
of saltpeter and of sulfur, and you could enclose one end of a tube,

463
00:25:47,941 --> 00:25:51,940
that gunpowder would be able to push out

464
00:25:51,941 --> 00:25:55,280
and a projectile.

465
00:25:55,380 --> 00:25:58,340
That was the basic principle behind the cannon,

466
00:25:58,440 --> 00:26:01,220
the first explosive siege machine developed

467
00:26:01,320 --> 00:26:02,620
in the 13th century.

468
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:04,820
First cannons were pretty crude.

469
00:26:04,920 --> 00:26:09,800
They looked a bit like big, lumpy vases laid on their sides

470
00:26:09,900 --> 00:26:15,100
with a flared muzzle and a fat chamber to put the powder in.

471
00:26:15,200 --> 00:26:18,160
And they were very inefficient.

472
00:26:18,260 --> 00:26:20,060
The early cannons were inefficient

473
00:26:20,061 --> 00:26:23,100
because engineers had yet to harness the explosive nature

474
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:26,000
of gunpowder.

475
00:26:26,100 --> 00:26:30,140
Gunpowder is very slow burning.

476
00:26:30,240 --> 00:26:33,100
You have to pack it tight to make it explosive.

477
00:26:33,200 --> 00:26:35,840
In so doing, you deprive it of air.

478
00:26:35,940 --> 00:26:39,480
So the gunpowder itself burns very slowly,

479
00:26:39,580 --> 00:26:43,280
a bit like a firework in the bottom of these tubes,

480
00:26:43,380 --> 00:26:46,620
and therefore projects the missile very badly.

481
00:26:46,621 --> 00:26:50,860
So what looks flash, bang, smoke, pretty spectacular.

482
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:52,860
Actually, even if you're on the receiving end,

483
00:26:52,960 --> 00:26:56,860
isn't that effective and certainly isn't very accurate.

484
00:26:56,960 --> 00:26:59,360
Even though the early cannons were inaccurate,

485
00:26:59,460 --> 00:27:02,260
they were used alongside catapults and trebuchets

486
00:27:02,360 --> 00:27:07,180
at the siege of Tournai in 1340, and again at the siege of Calais

487
00:27:07,280 --> 00:27:11,880
in 1346 because they were intimidating to defenders.

488
00:27:11,980 --> 00:27:14,780
It took nearly 200 years to realize

489
00:27:14,781 --> 00:27:17,420
that if they granulated gunpowder,

490
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:21,360
that is, they mixed the powder in the correct ratios,

491
00:27:21,460 --> 00:27:27,260
then soaked it, usually in human urine, then dried it,

492
00:27:27,360 --> 00:27:28,700
it dries into a cake.

493
00:27:28,800 --> 00:27:31,500
You break the cake up, and you get granules.

494
00:27:31,600 --> 00:27:35,940
The granules allow plenty of air in the charge,

495
00:27:36,040 --> 00:27:39,440
and the gun becomes much more efficient.

496
00:27:39,540 --> 00:27:42,500
By the 15th century, cannons

497
00:27:42,501 --> 00:27:47,740
Cannons became an effective siege machine.

498
00:27:47,840 --> 00:27:51,040
Cannons can begin to bring down walls of fortifications,

499
00:27:51,140 --> 00:27:53,140
and fortification consequently have to develop.

500
00:27:55,840 --> 00:28:00,460
In Scotland, King James II had a cannon called Mons Meg

501
00:28:00,560 --> 00:28:06,800
that fired a 19 and 1⁄2-inch iron ball 1,400 yards.

502
00:28:06,900 --> 00:28:09,060
This was the beginning of the end for high-walled

503
00:28:09,160 --> 00:28:11,900
fortifications.

504
00:28:11,901 --> 00:28:16,140
This castle behind us stood a several-month-long siege

505
00:28:16,240 --> 00:28:21,480
in the 17th century against very effective cannons,

506
00:28:21,580 --> 00:28:25,180
because the sheer weight of masonry here

507
00:28:25,280 --> 00:28:26,520
is still very effective.

508
00:28:29,720 --> 00:28:33,160
But castles like Carfilly were rare.

509
00:28:33,260 --> 00:28:35,820
By the 17th century, improvements in casting

510
00:28:35,920 --> 00:28:38,500
techniques and the standardization of the iron

511
00:28:38,501 --> 00:28:43,240
helped make the cannon the definitive siege machine.

512
00:28:43,340 --> 00:28:46,740
Not only did the cannon change the nature of siege warfare,

513
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:50,740
but it also changed the face of fortifications forever.

514
00:28:50,840 --> 00:28:56,060
They began to make castles so that they could contain

515
00:28:56,160 --> 00:28:58,260
batteries of guns.

516
00:28:58,360 --> 00:29:02,660
And they developed this distinctive form, the star.

517
00:29:02,760 --> 00:29:07,460
They built castles in a different way, rather low walls,

518
00:29:07,461 --> 00:29:11,460
rather deep ditches in front of them, multiple ditches,

519
00:29:11,560 --> 00:29:16,060
cleared areas around them to give fields of fire.

520
00:29:16,160 --> 00:29:20,100
Garrisons became bigger, and they had lots and lots of cannon

521
00:29:20,200 --> 00:29:23,380
in them, big cannon as well, so that they could outrange

522
00:29:23,480 --> 00:29:25,640
the enemy.

523
00:29:25,740 --> 00:29:27,540
But not even these fortifications

524
00:29:27,640 --> 00:29:29,980
could withstand the ever-increasing accuracy

525
00:29:30,080 --> 00:29:33,480
and power of cannon fire.

526
00:29:33,580 --> 00:29:36,120
A siege that used to take years now

527
00:29:36,121 --> 00:29:39,820
now took only months, or days.

528
00:29:39,920 --> 00:29:41,820
Becoming too expensive to rebuild,

529
00:29:41,920 --> 00:29:43,920
high-walled fortifications were finally

530
00:29:44,020 --> 00:29:46,160
robbed of their value.

531
00:29:46,260 --> 00:29:48,660
High, thick walls as a means of defense

532
00:29:48,760 --> 00:29:51,360
hit the dirt for good, and with it,

533
00:29:51,460 --> 00:29:54,280
one of the most essential obstacles in siege warfare.

534
00:30:00,080 --> 00:30:03,020
By the late 19th and early 20th century,

535
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:05,580
excavated earth replaced iron and stone

536
00:30:05,581 --> 00:30:08,580
as the materials of choice to fortify defenses in war.

537
00:30:11,580 --> 00:30:14,580
In World War I, these defenses went underground

538
00:30:14,680 --> 00:30:18,160
as siege warfare turned into trench warfare.

539
00:30:18,260 --> 00:30:21,420
And with it, a new siege engine emerged.

540
00:30:21,520 --> 00:30:24,260
Tanks were certainly conceived as a siege engine.

541
00:30:24,360 --> 00:30:27,700
The whole idea was that the machine gunners couldn't stop it,

542
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:29,500
and the machine gunners would be the problem

543
00:30:29,600 --> 00:30:32,380
once the barrage lifted and you tried to make your attack.

544
00:30:32,480 --> 00:30:34,100
It could cross trenches.

545
00:30:34,101 --> 00:30:38,600
It's designed to be just big enough to go right over trenches.

546
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:41,800
The first tanks able to breach sandbagged, barbed-wired,

547
00:30:41,900 --> 00:30:45,220
entrenched troops went into action in the Battle of Sum

548
00:30:45,320 --> 00:30:48,120
on September 15, 1916.

549
00:30:48,220 --> 00:30:50,580
They were essentially armored farm tractors.

550
00:30:50,680 --> 00:30:55,780
They put armor plate on it and cannons or machine

551
00:30:55,880 --> 00:30:58,800
guns on the thing.

552
00:30:58,900 --> 00:31:02,260
And it had a crew of eight men.

553
00:31:02,261 --> 00:31:05,960
Developed by the British Army, this 28-ton siege engine

554
00:31:06,060 --> 00:31:10,400
traveled at a top speed of almost 4 miles an hour

555
00:31:10,500 --> 00:31:12,740
and crossed 10-foot trenches.

556
00:31:12,840 --> 00:31:16,200
It was also equipped with two 57-millimeter cannons.

557
00:31:16,300 --> 00:31:19,580
The tank is essentially a moving gun platform.

558
00:31:19,680 --> 00:31:25,880
It allows you to have a varied caliber of weapon in a vehicle

559
00:31:25,980 --> 00:31:29,780
moving along and targeting fixed positions.

560
00:31:32,260 --> 00:31:34,820
early tanks were fickle mechanically.

561
00:31:34,821 --> 00:31:37,700
But employed in mass, they gained increasing success

562
00:31:37,701 --> 00:31:41,200
with accompanying infantry and artillery support.

563
00:31:41,201 --> 00:31:44,780
But they only had a range of 23 miles.

564
00:31:44,781 --> 00:31:48,880
By World War II, you could go a lot faster and a lot further.

565
00:31:48,881 --> 00:31:51,940
By the way, also, your stuff didn't break down as much.

566
00:31:51,941 --> 00:31:54,540
That changed it a lot.

567
00:31:54,541 --> 00:31:56,680
In World War II, the tanks set in motion

568
00:31:56,681 --> 00:32:00,480
the unlikely collaboration of historic opponents,

569
00:32:00,481 --> 00:32:03,620
siege machines, and fortifications.

570
00:32:03,621 --> 00:32:08,100
In essence, the tank became an effective, movable fortress.

571
00:32:08,101 --> 00:32:14,720
It allows you to go to the enemy with a large amount

572
00:32:14,721 --> 00:32:16,200
of firepower.

573
00:32:16,201 --> 00:32:21,360
And the mobility of the tank allows

574
00:32:21,361 --> 00:32:24,480
you not to have to build these large fortifications anymore.

575
00:32:27,260 --> 00:32:29,420
In the late 20th century, there

576
00:32:29,421 --> 00:32:33,120
in siege machine technology and design.

577
00:32:33,220 --> 00:32:36,220
Up until then, siege machines had absolutely no control

578
00:32:36,320 --> 00:32:38,160
over the projectiles once fired.

579
00:32:41,560 --> 00:32:46,140
All of the projectiles were, in military terms, dumb.

580
00:32:46,240 --> 00:32:48,540
Projectiles got smart and weapons got smart

581
00:32:48,640 --> 00:32:51,600
with the introduction of radar and more sophisticated

582
00:32:51,700 --> 00:32:53,080
navigation systems.

583
00:32:53,180 --> 00:32:56,540
The next big leap up came with the introduction

584
00:32:56,541 --> 00:32:58,840
the introduction of the GPS satellite system,

585
00:32:58,940 --> 00:33:02,340
those 24 orbiting satellites that could directly guide

586
00:33:02,440 --> 00:33:05,240
a missile or other components to the opponent.

587
00:33:05,340 --> 00:33:10,480
At essence, any weapon are two things, energy and accuracy.

588
00:33:10,580 --> 00:33:12,680
The energy part hasn't changed much.

589
00:33:12,780 --> 00:33:16,060
What's changed over the years fundamentally is accuracy.

590
00:33:16,160 --> 00:33:19,460
Each year, each month, smart weapons get smarter

591
00:33:19,560 --> 00:33:20,960
and that makes them more accurate.

592
00:33:23,660 --> 00:33:25,800
Computer technology fuels today's

593
00:33:25,801 --> 00:33:29,100
arsenal of smart weapons, from remote-controlled unmanned

594
00:33:29,200 --> 00:33:37,140
combat vehicles to airborne laser systems and cruise

595
00:33:37,240 --> 00:33:40,440
missiles, the quintessential siege machine.

596
00:33:40,540 --> 00:33:43,460
Launched from as far away as 1,000 miles,

597
00:33:43,560 --> 00:33:46,460
cruise missiles travel 500 miles per hour,

598
00:33:46,560 --> 00:33:49,860
hugging low terrain to avoid detection.

599
00:33:49,960 --> 00:33:53,460
Today's cruise missile is at the end of a long line

600
00:33:53,461 --> 00:33:55,800
of classic siege weapons.

601
00:33:55,900 --> 00:33:59,400
It still conveys a lot of energy, but it's far more accurate.

602
00:33:59,500 --> 00:34:01,500
And of course, can go greater distances.

603
00:34:01,600 --> 00:34:03,540
But like the classical weapons, they're very difficult

604
00:34:03,640 --> 00:34:05,180
to defend against.

605
00:34:05,280 --> 00:34:07,680
But unlike those, they're increasingly accurate.

606
00:34:12,640 --> 00:34:16,620
The smart projectile is now a universal siege machine,

607
00:34:16,720 --> 00:34:19,220
capable of breaching countries and continents

608
00:34:19,320 --> 00:34:21,620
anywhere on the planet.

609
00:34:21,621 --> 00:34:24,660
The cost of cruise missiles continues to fall,

610
00:34:24,760 --> 00:34:26,800
and more countries are acquiring them.

611
00:34:26,900 --> 00:34:28,960
And the trouble is, there's no good way

612
00:34:29,060 --> 00:34:30,800
to defend against a cruise missile

613
00:34:30,900 --> 00:34:32,000
once it's launched at you.

614
00:34:32,100 --> 00:34:34,360
It is like trying to knock down a bullet with a bullet.

615
00:34:34,460 --> 00:34:38,000
That is a fundamentally destabilizing force

616
00:34:38,100 --> 00:34:39,300
in modern warfare.

617
00:34:41,740 --> 00:34:43,800
While the age of formal siege warfare

618
00:34:43,900 --> 00:34:47,980
is long past, the development of siege weapons continues.

619
00:34:48,080 --> 00:34:51,380
Are we as a species hardwired to attack and attack

620
00:34:51,381 --> 00:34:54,480
and destroy fortifications of all kinds just

621
00:34:54,580 --> 00:34:56,480
because they are there?

622
00:34:56,580 --> 00:34:59,620
You just can't dispute that we are an adversarial species.

623
00:34:59,720 --> 00:35:03,660
We do value our safety and protect ourselves.

624
00:35:03,760 --> 00:35:05,860
And so it's part of the human condition

625
00:35:05,960 --> 00:35:08,460
that we will always differ with each other.

626
00:35:08,560 --> 00:35:09,860
We will always fight each other.

627
00:35:09,960 --> 00:35:12,060
And we will always defend ourselves against each other.

628
00:35:12,160 --> 00:35:14,800
The real legacy of all the siege stuff

629
00:35:14,900 --> 00:35:18,500
is that every time people thought that had been decided

630
00:35:18,600 --> 00:35:20,980
in the favor of the defenders or the attackers,

631
00:35:20,981 --> 00:35:23,380
permanently turned out to be a very unpleasant surprise

632
00:35:23,480 --> 00:35:26,380
for someone.

633
00:35:26,480 --> 00:35:28,920
And maybe that's the most important lesson of all,

634
00:35:29,020 --> 00:35:31,120
that these things are not permanent,

635
00:35:31,220 --> 00:35:33,420
and it's much more a cyclical thing.

636
00:35:33,520 --> 00:35:34,720
It goes around.

637
00:35:34,820 --> 00:35:39,540
I think the notion that it boils down to men over technology

638
00:35:39,640 --> 00:35:42,500
is as true today as it was then.

639
00:35:42,600 --> 00:35:45,040
And I think that that is what we have to remember.

640
00:35:45,140 --> 00:35:50,980
Military technology is always dependent upon that person

641
00:35:50,981 --> 00:35:56,320
who is developing it and who is also utilizing it and also

642
00:35:56,321 --> 00:35:57,220
defending against it.

643
00:36:02,460 --> 00:36:04,220
While the development of siege machines

644
00:36:04,221 --> 00:36:06,960
has historically been a deadly business,

645
00:36:06,961 --> 00:36:09,260
it has also been one of resourcefulness

646
00:36:09,261 --> 00:36:11,500
and inventiveness.

647
00:36:11,501 --> 00:36:14,400
It's those characteristics that typify the siege engine

648
00:36:14,401 --> 00:36:15,680
spin-offs of today.

649
00:36:15,681 --> 00:36:22,680
There is no place on Earth that demonstrates the power,

650
00:36:22,681 --> 00:36:33,640
grace, and crazy ingenuity of modern-day siege machines,

651
00:36:33,641 --> 00:36:37,040
more than the World Championship Pumpkin Chunking Contest.

652
00:36:37,041 --> 00:36:39,540
You can't say pumpkin chunking without a smile on your face.

653
00:36:39,541 --> 00:36:41,040
That's right.

654
00:36:41,041 --> 00:36:44,040
Pumpkin chunking is to these would-be warriors,

655
00:36:44,041 --> 00:36:47,780
what firing 200-pound rocks was to their medieval counterparts.

656
00:36:51,680 --> 00:36:54,580
Once you start throwing stuff, you just have to go and throw

657
00:36:54,680 --> 00:36:56,080
some more stuff with different machines

658
00:36:56,180 --> 00:36:57,480
and keep building stuff.

659
00:36:57,580 --> 00:36:59,880
There's just no going back.

660
00:36:59,980 --> 00:37:01,800
Pumpkin chunking began innocently enough

661
00:37:01,900 --> 00:37:05,720
in rural Delaware in 1986, evolving out of a barroom

662
00:37:05,820 --> 00:37:09,700
duel between Big Bill Thompson and a couple of his friends

663
00:37:09,800 --> 00:37:14,040
over the designs they fashioned on cocktail napkins.

664
00:37:14,041 --> 00:37:21,580
Draw machines on napkins and stuff like that and threaten that you can beat the other guy

665
00:37:21,581 --> 00:37:26,000
and then we set it up for a date the week after Halloween, come out high noon and challenge

666
00:37:26,001 --> 00:37:28,280
you with your best machine.

667
00:37:28,281 --> 00:37:33,440
Just started out for fun.

668
00:37:33,441 --> 00:37:39,420
There was only three machines that were out there playing at the time and the furthest

669
00:37:39,421 --> 00:37:42,080
throw was 178 feet.

670
00:37:42,081 --> 00:37:46,080
Today, the annual contest draws 30,000 spectators

671
00:37:46,180 --> 00:37:49,080
and features more than 60 different contraptions,

672
00:37:49,180 --> 00:37:52,420
all of them based loosely on one kind of medieval siege

673
00:37:52,520 --> 00:37:53,420
machine or another.

674
00:37:57,520 --> 00:38:01,340
We don't care really what time of history it came from.

675
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:05,140
We take whatever's good from whenever

676
00:38:05,240 --> 00:38:08,400
and try and use and build the medieval machines stronger

677
00:38:08,500 --> 00:38:09,540
and more accurately.

678
00:38:09,640 --> 00:38:11,440
This is a regulator.

679
00:38:11,441 --> 00:38:13,440
It's an ancient trebuchet.

680
00:38:13,441 --> 00:38:17,440
It's an old wartime machine, is what it's prototype after.

681
00:38:17,441 --> 00:38:21,440
And it's made of scrap steel that we found out here in the yard.

682
00:38:21,441 --> 00:38:24,440
It's actually a burnt down trailer that I cut in half.

683
00:38:24,441 --> 00:38:28,440
And before that, it was the trailer I lived in.

684
00:38:28,441 --> 00:38:31,440
But we thought it was a nice frame.

685
00:38:31,441 --> 00:38:33,440
Put a catapult on.

686
00:38:33,441 --> 00:38:35,440
Fire in the hole!

687
00:38:41,440 --> 00:38:46,440
In 1996, we started on a boat trailer with small garage door

688
00:38:46,441 --> 00:38:51,440
springs and a very weak beam.

689
00:38:51,441 --> 00:38:54,440
And I think our best throw was 50 feet.

690
00:38:54,441 --> 00:38:55,440
The Boy Scouts beat us that year.

691
00:38:55,441 --> 00:38:59,440
So we went back with our tail between the legs.

692
00:38:59,441 --> 00:39:03,440
John Huber, an engineer at a local nuclear station,

693
00:39:03,441 --> 00:39:07,440
decided to build a machine with the help of his computer at work,

694
00:39:07,441 --> 00:39:10,940
one that would blow the Boy Scouts out of the water.

695
00:39:11,040 --> 00:39:14,040
We loaded this whole thing in CAD and broke it five times

696
00:39:14,140 --> 00:39:17,180
before we got all the weld geometry right and the materials.

697
00:39:17,280 --> 00:39:19,380
And there are welds on this machine that

698
00:39:19,480 --> 00:39:22,880
see 196,000 foot pounds of force.

699
00:39:22,980 --> 00:39:24,200
Firefighter.

700
00:39:24,300 --> 00:39:26,000
To demonstrate, Huber spray painted

701
00:39:26,100 --> 00:39:28,800
a pumpkin fluorescent pink so our camera could follow it

702
00:39:28,900 --> 00:39:29,900
in flight.

703
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:35,400
We saw it launch, but we never saw it land.

704
00:39:35,401 --> 00:39:38,400
And for all we know, the pink pumpkin is still up there.

705
00:39:38,500 --> 00:39:40,400
That baby went out there.

706
00:39:44,400 --> 00:39:47,640
For the contest, the rules are simple.

707
00:39:47,740 --> 00:39:50,940
Pumpkins must weigh between 8 and 10 pounds,

708
00:39:51,040 --> 00:39:55,060
leave the machine intact, and not be propelled by explosives.

709
00:39:59,560 --> 00:40:01,960
After that, you can pretty much figure

710
00:40:01,961 --> 00:40:05,500
figure that one man's junk is another man's siege engine

711
00:40:05,501 --> 00:40:07,500
prototype. There's no prize money

712
00:40:07,501 --> 00:40:10,000
involved. Just the bragging rights, the right

713
00:40:10,001 --> 00:40:12,000
to say you're the world champion pumpkin chunker.

714
00:40:12,001 --> 00:40:15,700
I was the first one to ever try plastic pipe, and it worked.

715
00:40:15,701 --> 00:40:18,000
It didn't blow up my face or anything.

716
00:40:18,001 --> 00:40:22,920
And we cranked that sucker up and put a pumpkin in it, and it

717
00:40:22,921 --> 00:40:28,620
went 2,655 feet. Those are bragging rights.

718
00:40:28,621 --> 00:40:31,660
And that was the world record for an eight-pound pumpkin

719
00:40:31,760 --> 00:40:37,360
pitched from this modified air cannon back in 1995.

720
00:40:37,460 --> 00:40:39,840
While chunking a pumpkin the farthest usually captures

721
00:40:39,940 --> 00:40:47,280
the crowd's attention, there's nothing quite like a siege

722
00:40:47,380 --> 00:40:48,680
machine with accuracy.

723
00:40:48,780 --> 00:40:50,180
We haven't hit anything yet.

724
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:52,280
Actually, no, yesterday we did hit that truck.

725
00:40:52,380 --> 00:40:54,180
We hit her twice in one shot.

726
00:40:58,620 --> 00:41:05,620
Using his allowance money and donated scrap metal,

727
00:41:05,621 --> 00:41:08,620
14-year-old Jake Burton and his buddies assembled one of the

728
00:41:08,621 --> 00:41:13,620
most accurate air cannons ever to wage war on an old Chevy,

729
00:41:13,621 --> 00:41:16,620
abandoned at the end of the pasture.

730
00:41:16,621 --> 00:41:21,620
We fill this up with air, pressure about between,

731
00:41:21,621 --> 00:41:23,620
well, it depends how heavy your pumpkin is,

732
00:41:23,621 --> 00:41:25,620
and then the other one.

733
00:41:25,621 --> 00:41:28,380
I mean, it depends how heavy your pumpkin is,

734
00:41:28,381 --> 00:41:31,360
and then that depends how much air you need

735
00:41:31,361 --> 00:41:32,860
and how far you want to shoot it.

736
00:41:38,860 --> 00:41:40,500
We can shoot pretty much anything that

737
00:41:40,501 --> 00:41:45,740
fits in the 8-inch barrel and that isn't too heavy.

738
00:41:45,741 --> 00:41:51,240
And we shot up to 2,300 feet around there.

739
00:41:51,241 --> 00:41:52,980
Unlike soldiers in the Middle Ages

740
00:41:52,981 --> 00:41:57,180
siege machines to break down fixed defenses, chunkers just

741
00:41:57,280 --> 00:42:00,180
can't resist the simple pleasure and irresistible

742
00:42:00,280 --> 00:42:04,520
power of flinging something, anything through the air with

743
00:42:04,620 --> 00:42:06,820
their ingenious devices.

744
00:42:06,920 --> 00:42:09,220
I've seen toilets thrown.

745
00:42:09,320 --> 00:42:11,340
We've seen beer kegs thrown.

746
00:42:11,440 --> 00:42:13,440
We've seen watermelons, cabbage.

747
00:42:13,540 --> 00:42:15,340
Coleslaw coming up.

748
00:42:15,440 --> 00:42:18,980
Cantaloupes, lettuce, pumpkins, of course,

749
00:42:19,080 --> 00:42:20,080
white and yellow.

750
00:42:22,980 --> 00:42:23,880
Whoo!

751
00:42:27,880 --> 00:42:29,880
You make somebody laugh, you got them.

752
00:42:29,881 --> 00:42:31,180
There's not enough laughing going on.

753
00:42:31,181 --> 00:42:33,980
You got to let your hair down and play and enjoy life a little bit.

754
00:42:33,981 --> 00:42:37,380
And that's what this is about, the whole festival.

755
00:42:37,381 --> 00:42:41,080
It's ironic that machines that once destroyed entire cities

756
00:42:41,081 --> 00:42:44,280
and displaced thousands now draw a crowd.

757
00:42:47,780 --> 00:42:51,280
From time immemorial to the 21st century,

758
00:42:51,281 --> 00:42:53,280
there has been an enduring fascination

759
00:42:53,380 --> 00:42:57,580
with the primitive mechanics and brute strength of siege

760
00:42:57,680 --> 00:42:59,180
machines.

761
00:43:51,280 --> 00:43:53,280
You

